<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moktar Ould Daddah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1924">1924</a>–<a href="/years/2003">2003</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Moktar Ould Daddah was the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, from independence on 28 November 1960 until 1978. A lawyer trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, he became head of government in 1957, oversaw the building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nouakchott">Nouakchott</a> as the new capital and later made his People's Party the sole legal party. The costly war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polisario-front">Polisario Front</a> led to his overthrow by a military coup in July 1978.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moktar-ould-daddah.jpg" alt="Moktar Ould Daddah" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moktar_Ould_Daddah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Moktar Ould Daddah was born on 25 December 1924 into an important marabout family of the Ouled Birri tribe in Boutilimit, in French West Africa. After attending elite Islamic academies he worked for the French colonial administrators as a translator. He studied law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, becoming the first Mauritanian to hold a university degree, and was admitted to the bar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a> in 1955.</p><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> in the late 1950s he joined the Mauritanian Progressive Union. In 1959 he founded the Mauritanian Regroupment Party, which won every seat in the National Assembly in the last pre-independence elections that year, and he was appointed prime minister. He was known for building consensus among the White Moors, Black Moors and Black Africans, Mauritania's three main ethnic groups, and the French granted independence in 1960.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Named acting president of the new Islamic Republic, Moktar was confirmed in the first post-independence election in August 1961. In December the four largest parties merged as the Mauritanian People's Party, which became the sole legal party, and a constitution of 1964 formalised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>, which he justified by saying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> was unready for Western-style multi-party democracy. He was re-elected in uncontested elections in 1966, 1971 and 1976. He followed a policy of Islamic socialism with many nationalisations, joined the Non-Aligned Movement, kept strong links with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> and accepted French aid. In 1971 he was president of the Organization of African Unity.</p><p>Drought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> from 1969 to 1974 and falling iron prices lowered living standards. In 1975 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in the attempt to annex <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a>, which Moktar had claimed as part of Greater Mauritania since 1957. After the Madrid Accords with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> it annexed a southern portion, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polisario-front">Polisario Front</a>'s guerrillas defeated the small Mauritanian Army, attacked the iron mines at Zouerate and in 1976 attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nouakchott">Nouakchott</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 10 July 1978 a military coup led by Mustafa Ould Salek overthrew him. His successors withdrew from the war in August 1979. Moktar went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> that month after a period of imprisonment, and founded the opposition Alliance pour une Mauritanie Démocratique in 1980. He was allowed to return on 17 July 2001, and died after a long illness in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 14 October 2003. He was buried at the El Balatiya cemetery near Boutilimit. In his memoirs he expressed concern that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> could lead to an armed conflict that would destroy the country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Moktar Ould Daddah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mauritania became an autonomous Islamic Republic in 1958 and independent on 28 November 1960 under <b>Moktar Ould Daddah</b>, despite Moroccan claims to its territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-mauritania">French Mauritania</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Moktar Ould Daddah</b>, originally installed by the French, became the first president.</blockquote><a href="/state/mauritania">Mauritania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Boutilimit, in what is now <a href="/countries/mauritania">Mauritania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.55,-14.69&amp;year=1975" aria-label="Boutilimit on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Moktar Ould Daddah"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3815 5980h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3815 5980h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.55,-14.69&amp;year=1975">What was here in 1975? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
